<section class="hero">
  <img class="mark" src="icon.svg" alt="" width="88" height="88">
  <h1>Find what is tangled.</h1>
  <p class="lede">
    <strong>thicket</strong> reads your TypeScript and writes a report. It lists repeated code
    and circular imports, and says where each one is. You give the report to a coding agent.
    thicket does not grade your code, and it does not edit it.
  </p>
  <div class="cta">
    <a class="primary" href="#install">Install and run</a>
    <a href="report-guide.html">How to read a report</a>
    <a href="example.html">See an example</a>
    <a href="https://github.com/alecf/thicket">GitHub</a>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="prose">
  <h2 id="install">Install and run</h2>
  <pre><code class="language-bash">brew install alecf/tap/thicket

thicket &gt; thicket.md</code></pre>
  <p>
    Run it from your project root. thicket finds the tsconfig files on its own. In a monorepo
    it covers every workspace. Use <code>--filter</code> to pick some of them. Use
    <code>--config</code> to name the tsconfig files yourself.
  </p>
  <p>
    You can also download a tarball from
    <a href="https://github.com/alecf/thicket/releases">Releases</a>. Unpack it and put
    <code>thicket</code> on your <code>PATH</code>. A symlink works. macOS and Linux are
    prebuilt, for arm64 and x64.
  </p>
  <blockquote>
    <p>
      The tarball unpacks to a folder, not a single file. It holds the binary and the
      <code>tsgo/</code> compiler that thicket runs. Keep them together.
    </p>
  </blockquote>

  <h2 id="then-what">What to do with the report</h2>
  <p>
    Give it to an LLM. thicket finds the candidates. The agent decides which ones to fix.
  </p>

  <h3 id="hand-it-over">1. Run it yourself</h3>
  <pre><code class="language-bash">thicket &gt; thicket.md</code></pre>
  <p>Then ask an agent to read the file:</p>
  <blockquote>
    <p>
      Read <code>thicket.md</code>. It lists duplication and dependency-cycle candidates, and it
      has not judged them. The top of the file links to a guide. Fetch that first. Pick the
      findings worth acting on. Tell me what you rejected and why. Then do the top one.
    </p>
  </blockquote>
  <p>
    Every report links to the <a href="report-guide.md">field guide</a>, as raw Markdown. The
    agent fetches it and learns what every field means.
  </p>

  <h3 id="agent-runs-it">2. Or let the agent run it</h3>
  <blockquote>
    <p>
      Run <code>thicket &gt; thicket.md</code> and read the report. Pick the one finding you are
      most sure about, and fix it. Then re-run thicket and show me that the finding is gone.
    </p>
  </blockquote>
  <p>
    If you do this often, put the command in your <code>CLAUDE.md</code> or
    <code>AGENTS.md</code>. Then the agent already knows it.
  </p>

  <h3 id="close-the-loop">3. Check the work</h3>
  <pre><code class="language-bash">thicket --json before.json &gt; thicket.md
# ...the agent refactors...
thicket --json after.json &gt; thicket.md
thicket diff before.json after.json</code></pre>
  <p>
    A finding gets its ID from the code itself, not from where the code sits. So the diff shows
    you what the agent really fixed. Code that only moved keeps its ID.
  </p>

  <h2 id="what-it-reports">What it reports</h2>
</section>

<section class="cards">
  <div class="card">
    <h3>Duplication</h3>
    <p>
      Repeated code, grouped and ranked by how many lines you would delete. Each group shows
      what changes from one copy to the next.
    </p>
  </div>
  <div class="card">
    <h3>Module tangles</h3>
    <p>
      Modules that import each other, drawn as a chart. thicket also tells you if the files are
      really circular, or if it is just the folders.
    </p>
  </div>
  <div class="card">
    <h3>Cheap fixes first</h3>
    <p>
      Some imports pass through one file on the way from another. Point them at the real source
      and the link goes away. thicket lists those before it suggests cutting anything.
    </p>
  </div>
  <div class="card">
    <h3>Identical runs</h3>
    <p>
      Same code in, same report out, byte for byte. Diff two reports to see if your refactor
      worked.
    </p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="prose">
  <h2 id="not-a-linter">It is not a linter</h2>
  <p>thicket does not score your codebase. Nothing in the report is a defect.</p>
  <ul>
    <li>No pass or fail. It exits 0 whether it finds 3 findings or 18,000.</li>
    <li>The summary numbers track one codebase over time. They do not compare two codebases.</li>
    <li>
      Every finding is a candidate. Plenty are not worth fixing, and
      <a href="report-guide.html#is-this-duplication-worth-removing">the guide says how to tell</a>.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    thicket does the searching. The agent does the thinking. This is also the cheap way round.
    A model only sees the files it opens, and opening every file costs tokens for every file.
  </p>

  <h2 id="ai-written-code">Written by agents?</h2>
  <p>
    An agent writes what it can see. It misses the helper in a file it never opened, so it
    writes that helper again. Every diff looks fine on its own. The copies add up.
  </p>
  <p>
    thicket finds exactly that. It matches the same code across files, even when the names are
    different. Run it once a week and compare it to last week.
  </p>
  <pre><code class="language-bash">thicket --json .thicket/this-week.json &gt; thicket.md
thicket diff .thicket/last-week.json .thicket/this-week.json</code></pre>
  <p>Use it for cleanup. Do not turn it into a required check.</p>
</section>
